mip CSRRS/CSRRC must not latch the SEIP/STIP pins (#21)
Branch up-irq-mip-rmw on upstream main 4c5a51a, RTL commit cc2cfed.
Host: shared 48-core Linux box at load ~150-220. Wall times are
from that box and are inflated by the load.

Root cause
----------
CSRRS/CSRRC computed the new mip value as (rdata | src) or
(rdata & ~src). rdata for mip ORs irq_s_ext_i into SEIP and
irq_s_timer_i into STIP. If either pin was high during a mip RMW,
the RMW wrote 1 into the software-writable bit. OpenSBI does
csr_set/csr_clear(mip, MIP_STIP) on every timer event. Once
Linux's interrupt-driven UART tty was active (/init's first write),
the PLIC S-context line was often high at that moment. mip.SEIP then
stuck at 1, every PLIC claim returned 0, and Linux re-entered
the S-external handler forever (soc_tb boot livelock after
/init's uname line). Priv spec (mip): the RMW uses the software
SEIP bit only, and reads see pin | software bit.

Fix: muserisc_csr exports rmw_rdata_o. It equals rdata_o, except
that for mip, SEIP and STIP are the software bits. The core uses
it as the CSRRS(I)/CSRRC(I) base.
The RTL SoC (muserisc_soc_top) drives irq_s_ext_i from the PLIC
S context, so the bug and the fix apply to it as well as to soc_tb.

Regression tests and old-RTL check
----------------------------------
core_tb: stores to IRQ_CTL (0x10000008) drive the interrupt pins
(mip bit positions 3/5/7/9/11).
sw/core-tests/irq_seip_rmw.S and irq_mip_rmw_variants.S were built
against upstream 4c5a51a muserisc_csr.sv + muserisc_core.sv, with
everything else from this branch:
  CORE TB FAIL irq_seip_rmw.bin test 4 (57 cycles)
  CORE TB FAIL irq_mip_rmw_variants.bin test 3 (55 cycles)
With the fix:
  CORE TB PASS irq_seip_rmw.bin (167 cycles)
  CORE TB PASS irq_mip_rmw_variants.bin (179 cycles)
csr_tb: CSR TB PASS (214 checks), including rmw_rdata_o for mip
(pins high, software bits) and for a non-mip CSR.

